1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:04,774
An astronaut is floating outside the space station when his helmet begins to fill with water.

2
00:00:05,899 --> 00:00:08,988
Within minutes, it covers his eyes. Then his ears.

3
00:00:08,988 --> 00:00:15,852
He cannot see, he cannot hear, and he does not know if his next breath will be air, or water.

4
00:00:16,999 --> 00:00:20,936
He is a real astronaut. This is his real account, in his own words.

5
00:00:20,936 --> 00:00:26,560
And he is only one of twelve people on this list who lived through something that should have killed them.

6
00:00:27,699 --> 00:00:29,991
One honest rule before we start.

7
00:00:29,991 --> 00:00:37,248
Every quote in this video is real, on the record, from the astronaut's own memoir, interview, or mission transcript.

8
00:00:37,248 --> 00:00:38,775
Nothing here is invented.

9
00:00:39,899 --> 00:00:45,463
Number twelve. Andrew Feustel. Space Shuttle Endeavor's final flight, 2011.

10
00:00:46,600 --> 00:00:53,026
Mid spacewalk, soap used to stop his visor fogging flaked loose inside his helmet, and drifted into his eye.

11
00:00:54,166 --> 00:01:04,197
His real, live radio call to mission control: quote, just an F Y I, my right eye is stinging like crazy right now, it's watering a lot, end quote.

12
00:01:05,333 --> 00:01:10,110
He rubbed his eye against the padding inside his own helmet, and kept working.

13
00:01:10,110 --> 00:01:15,569
A NASA report later found this exact problem had happened on at least seven separate spacewalks.

14
00:01:15,569 --> 00:01:16,252
Still unsolved.

15
00:01:17,399 --> 00:01:22,388
Number eleven. Three astronauts. One capsule that came down completely wrong.

16
00:01:23,533 --> 00:01:24,323
April 2008.

17
00:01:24,323 --> 00:01:33,799
Peggy Whitson, Yuri Malenchenko, and Yi So Yeon are returning to Earth in a Soyuz capsule when its service module fails to detach cleanly.

18
00:01:34,933 --> 00:01:37,846
The capsule is forced into a steep, uncontrolled reentry.

19
00:01:37,846 --> 00:01:42,053
Instead of the normal four or five Gs, they hit eight point two.

20
00:01:42,053 --> 00:01:46,584
They land four hundred kilometers off target, in the middle of the Kazakh steppe.

21
00:01:47,733 --> 00:01:53,521
Yi So Yeon's own words: quote, I saw some kind of fire outside as we were going through the atmosphere.

22
00:01:53,521 --> 00:01:58,731
I thought we could burn. I looked at the others, and I pretended to be okay, end quote.

23
00:01:59,866 --> 00:02:05,273
Number ten. Apollo 12. Struck by lightning, twice, on the way up.

24
00:02:06,399 --> 00:02:15,096
Thirty six seconds after liftoff in November 1969, the Saturn Five rocket carrying Pete Conrad, Alan Bean, and Dick Gordon is struck by lightning.

25
00:02:15,096 --> 00:02:17,632
A second strike hits sixteen seconds later.

26
00:02:18,766 --> 00:02:22,399
Every warning light on the instrument panel fires at once.

27
00:02:22,399 --> 00:02:26,394
Conrad's own real words: quote, the hell was that, end quote.

28
00:02:27,533 --> 00:02:31,782
A ground engineer remembered one obscure switch from a training simulation months earlier.

29
00:02:31,782 --> 00:02:36,032
Alan Bean was the only person on board who remembered where it was.

30
00:02:36,032 --> 00:02:40,281
He flipped it, and the mission continued, all the way to the Moon.

31
00:02:41,400 --> 00:02:46,807
Number nine. The secret mission that foreshadowed a real disaster fifteen years early.

32
00:02:47,933 --> 00:02:48,712
December 1988.

33
00:02:48,712 --> 00:02:55,335
Eighty five seconds after launch, debris tears loose from the booster and slams into the shuttle's belly.

34
00:02:55,335 --> 00:03:01,569
Over 700 heat shield tiles are damaged. One is torn away completely, down to bare metal.

35
00:03:02,700 --> 00:03:10,542
Because the mission was classified, ground engineers saw only a blurry, compressed video feed, and badly underestimated the damage.

36
00:03:10,542 --> 00:03:17,146
Mission specialist Mike Mullane's own words, anticipating reentry: quote, we were dead men floating, end quote.

37
00:03:18,266 --> 00:03:26,743
During reentry, plasma actually melted through a steel plate directly beneath the missing tile, before the shuttle passed the worst of the heat.

38
00:03:26,743 --> 00:03:32,640
Commander Hoot Gibson's private thought, going in: quote, no use dying all tensed up, end quote.

39
00:03:32,640 --> 00:03:37,063
This is the exact same failure that destroyed Columbia, fifteen years later.

40
00:03:37,063 --> 00:03:38,537
This time, it held.

41
00:03:39,666 --> 00:03:40,376
Number eight.

42
00:03:40,376 --> 00:03:45,700
Two cosmonauts, watching a cargo ship on a collision course with their own space station.

43
00:03:46,833 --> 00:03:47,610
June 1997.

44
00:03:47,610 --> 00:03:56,159
Commander Vasily Tsibliyev is manually piloting an unmanned cargo ship toward a test docking, the automated system switched off to save money.

45
00:03:57,300 --> 00:04:04,790
Flight engineer Alexander Lazutkin's own words, watching it come in: quote, full of menace, like a shark, end quote.

46
00:04:04,790 --> 00:04:05,973
Then it hit.

47
00:04:07,100 --> 00:04:10,562
The cargo ship punched a hole clean through a research module.

48
00:04:10,562 --> 00:04:12,451
Air started rushing out into space.

49
00:04:12,451 --> 00:04:19,691
The crew had minutes to find the right cables, cut them by hand, and seal the hatch before the whole station lost pressure.

50
00:04:20,833 --> 00:04:21,616
Number seven.

51
00:04:21,616 --> 00:04:27,102
A docking with no instruments, and three lives riding on getting it exactly right.

52
00:04:28,233 --> 00:04:28,992
November 1995.

53
00:04:28,992 --> 00:04:36,200
Chris Hadfield's shuttle is thirty seconds from docking with the Russian station Mir when the docking sensors fail completely.

54
00:04:37,333 --> 00:04:41,039
The crew has to judge the final approach by eye, and by stopwatch.

55
00:04:41,039 --> 00:04:50,734
Hadfield's own words on what was at stake: quote, if you hit Mir just a little too hard, then you would break Mir in half, and kill the three people on board, end quote.

56
00:04:51,866 --> 00:04:57,993
Number six is the only story on this list with no camera, no footage, nothing.

57
00:04:57,993 --> 00:05:00,852
Just one man's word, for decades, unbelieved.

58
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:02,774
January 1969.

59
00:05:02,774 --> 00:05:08,582
Cosmonaut Boris Volynov is coming home from orbit when his service module fails to detach.

60
00:05:08,582 --> 00:05:12,841
His capsule reenters the atmosphere hatch first, not heat shield first.

61
00:05:13,966 --> 00:05:15,509
Smoke fills the cabin.

62
00:05:15,509 --> 00:05:23,997
He repeats one phrase to himself, over and over, certain he is about to die: quote, no panic, no panic, end quote.

63
00:05:25,133 --> 00:05:30,353
At the last possible moment, the burning service module tears away, and the capsule rights itself.

64
00:05:30,353 --> 00:05:34,268
The landing is still violent enough to break several of his teeth.

65
00:05:34,268 --> 00:05:39,814
He comes down two thousand kilometers off target, in the Ural Mountains, in temperatures of minus forty.

66
00:05:40,933 --> 00:05:49,026
His first words to the local villagers who found him, through a mouthful of broken teeth: quote, is my hair gray, end quote.

67
00:05:49,026 --> 00:05:52,897
The Soviet government suppressed the entire story for over twenty years.

68
00:05:54,033 --> 00:05:59,754
Number five. The first-ever docking in space, and the emergency that nearly ended it.

69
00:06:00,900 --> 00:06:01,667
March 1966.

70
00:06:01,667 --> 00:06:09,729
Neil Armstrong has just completed the first docking between two spacecraft in orbit, when the joined vehicles begin an unexplained roll.

71
00:06:10,866 --> 00:06:19,663
David Scott's real-time radio call: quote, we have serious problems here, we're tumbling end over end, we're disengaged from the Agena, end quote.

72
00:06:19,663 --> 00:06:25,782
The spin keeps accelerating, toward a full revolution every second, fast enough to black them out.

73
00:06:26,900 --> 00:06:33,677
Armstrong shuts down the entire malfunctioning system, and uses thrusters meant only for reentry to stabilize the capsule.

74
00:06:33,677 --> 00:06:37,819
Mission rules now force an immediate emergency splashdown in the Pacific.

75
00:06:38,966 --> 00:06:45,157
Number four. A torn solar wing, live electricity, and a spacesuit filled with pure oxygen.

76
00:06:46,300 --> 00:06:51,080
November 2007. A solar array on the space station has torn during retraction.

77
00:06:51,080 --> 00:06:55,861
There is no procedure for fixing a live, power generating panel in orbit.

78
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:05,594
Scott Parazynski rides a ninety foot robotic arm farther from an airlock than anyone had ever gone, to cut a snagged wire on the fully energized array by hand.

79
00:07:06,733 --> 00:07:13,002
His own words: quote, there was a real danger that we could do even worse damage to the space station.

80
00:07:13,002 --> 00:07:23,974
Then there was the potential risk to myself, because if there was any metal to metal connection, it could actually electrocute me, or cause ignition of the 100 percent oxygen in my spacesuit, end quote.

81
00:07:25,100 --> 00:07:25,776
Number three.

82
00:07:25,776 --> 00:07:31,866
The most famous space emergency in history, told through the man who almost never gets asked about it.

83
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:38,580
April 1970. An oxygen tank explodes on Apollo 13, crippling power, water, and air.

84
00:07:38,580 --> 00:07:43,762
The crew moves into the tiny lunar module to survive the trip home.

85
00:07:44,900 --> 00:07:47,981
Fred Haise is the only crew member still alive today.

86
00:07:47,981 --> 00:07:54,759
His own words about the cabin, which dropped close to freezing for days: quote, we did not have adequate clothing, end quote.

87
00:07:54,759 --> 00:07:58,457
He put on three pairs of underwear just to survive the cold.

88
00:07:59,600 --> 00:08:05,373
Commander Jim Lovell, who guided that freezing capsule home, died last year, at 97.

89
00:08:05,373 --> 00:08:11,146
Fred Haise, now 92, is the only person left who was in that spacecraft.

90
00:08:12,266 --> 00:08:15,740
Number two. Gene Cernan gave this one its name himself.

91
00:08:16,866 --> 00:08:21,020
June 1966. Only the third American spacewalk ever attempted.

92
00:08:21,020 --> 00:08:27,942
With no handholds and no way to brace himself, every movement sends Gene Cernan tumbling.

93
00:08:29,066 --> 00:08:33,685
His exertion overwhelms the suit's cooling system. His heart rate hits 180.

94
00:08:33,685 --> 00:08:40,227
His visor fogs completely, then partially freezes in the dark of Earth's shadow, leaving him almost blind.

95
00:08:40,227 --> 00:08:46,385
His own real radio words: quote, I don't think I'll make it that way, end quote.

96
00:08:47,533 --> 00:08:49,831
He loses thirteen pounds of body water.

97
00:08:49,831 --> 00:08:54,756
Commander Tom Stafford has to order him back inside before he can finish the job.

98
00:08:54,756 --> 00:09:00,337
Cernan later titled the whole chapter of his own memoir, quote, the spacewalk from hell, end quote.

99
00:09:00,337 --> 00:09:01,979
His own words, not ours.

100
00:09:03,100 --> 00:09:05,869
Number one is the astronaut we opened this video with.

101
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:07,759
July 2013.

102
00:09:07,759 --> 00:09:15,359
Luca Parmitano is an hour into a spacewalk when water starts pooling inside his helmet, from a malfunctioning cooling system.

103
00:09:16,500 --> 00:09:22,375
It covers his eyes first. Then his ears. Then it starts to reach his nose and mouth.

104
00:09:22,375 --> 00:09:26,177
He cannot see. He cannot hear his crewmate, or mission control.

105
00:09:26,177 --> 00:09:29,979
He has to navigate back to the airlock from memory alone.

106
00:09:31,100 --> 00:09:39,308
His own words afterward: quote, I experienced what it's like to be a goldfish in a fishbowl, from the point of view of the goldfish, end quote.

107
00:09:39,308 --> 00:09:45,389
A later NASA investigation found a smaller version of this exact leak had happened before, and nobody had escalated it.

108
00:09:46,533 --> 00:09:47,790
Twelve real people.

109
00:09:47,790 --> 00:09:56,172
Twelve real moments where the plan broke down, and something else, training, luck, or one remembered switch, kept them alive.

110
00:09:57,300 --> 00:10:04,858
Almost all of them are still alive today, still willing to talk about the worst moment of their careers, in their own words.

111
00:10:04,858 --> 00:10:07,488
That, honestly, is the part worth sitting with.

112
00:10:08,633 --> 00:10:14,911
If what happened up there scared you, wait until you see what happened to the astronauts who never made it home at all.

113
00:10:14,911 --> 00:10:16,822
That story is on this channel too.

114
00:10:16,822 --> 00:10:19,552
Subscribe to mindwired, so you don't miss what comes next.

